lol, thats most stupid assumption you could do; here are reasons:
- SCSI order may change from kernel to kernel for MANY reasons:
* order to detect SCSI cards
* how IDs are attributed to device by automated process (especially unexpectable
with the Alpha 1200 front plane)
- SCSI PCI card driver not compiled
- swapped cards
- auto swapping ressources by linux kernel due to configuration (cf a bit like 'swap
onboard device' for IDE)

What you know is that your HDD was sdc with the live CD ... does not mean at all it
is still sdc with your new kernel :)

sorry, you cant know until you spent weeks seeking those bugs.

Thats life.

btw, I did not even have the opportunity to go that far since the Install CD itself
does KP for me :)

I just know that from experience with Gentoo on x86, and Debian on 1200.

Just remember that the kernel may alter letters in many ways; especially, the CD can
discover the mother board chain first (due to udev, putting the HDD of front pannel
on sdc, and your kernel may detect the pannel first due to linking of drivers inside
the kernel.

just try any
root=sda
=sdb
=sdc
=sdd (even you think it impossible, still try it).

