> make[2]: Entering directory
> /usr/include/c++/3.2.3/bits/basic_string.h:974:   instantiated from `bool
> std::operator==(const std::basic_string<_CharT, _Traits, _Alloc>&, const
> std::basic_string<_CharT, _Traits, _Alloc>&) [with _CharT = char, _Traits =
> std::char_traits<char>, _Alloc = std::allocator<char>]'
> ../lib/util.h:66:   instantiated from here
> /usr/include/c++/3.2.3/bits/basic_string.h:894: call of overloaded `min(
>    size_t&, size_t&)' is ambiguous
> ../lib/std_fixes.h:35: candidates are: T std::min(const T&, const T&) [with
> T =
>    size_t]
> /usr/include/c++/3.2.3/bits/stl_algobase.h:149:                 const _Tp&
>    std::min(const _Tp&, const _Tp&) [with _Tp = size_t]

(without having played a lot with templates)
Looks like the BOINC source is redefining the min algorithm to return a
non-const value in ../lib/std_fixes.h:35. Not sure why the compiler can't
choose between the 2 in either case, but anything is possible I guess in
the mess that's templates.

The canonical declarations for min (STL Tutorial & Reference Guide, 2nd
Ed, Musser/Serge/Dani) would appear to be:

template <typename T>
const T& min( const T& a, const T& b);

template <typename T, typename Compare>
const T& min( const T& a, const T& b, Compare comp);

Gut guess is to comment out the re-define (as well as it's code), and see
if that works.


